					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/petrograd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(455145); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=455145&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609448880" alt="Petrograd" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=455145)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/petrograd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(455145); return false;" >Petrograd</a></span> <span class="odate time_1473725225 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">13 Sep 2016 00:07</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2614829">
									

<p>Ok, so this is well-written and interesting. I really do enjoy the canary-in-the-coalmine effect. Unfortunately, there's a downside.</p>
<p>The flies are basically sensitive to &quot;SCP objects&quot;, which implies that either all anomalies have a common origin or mechanism or at least all alter reality using the extremely broad list of ways you provided. That's a controversial stance among the continuity.</p>
<p>You don't talk about failure rate or what percentage of known SCPs these things are or aren't sensitive to. It just makes them too damn convenient. Want to know if an object is anomalous? Stick it in a room with the bugs. Want to find every single uncontained SCP? Walk around with the bugs. It's the same problem people had with Kant Counters, but in a biological form even more powerful.<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-275446-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-275446-1')">1</a></sup></p>
<p>No vote. I actually enjoy Scranton Reality Anchors and occasionally am down with Humes, but even to me this just seems too powerful.</p>
<div class="footnotes-footer">
<div class="title">Footnotes</div>
<div class="footnote-footer" id="footnote-275446-1"><a href="javascript:;" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnoteref-275446-1')">1</a>. Edit: if you weren't around for that, there was a pretty big controversy on the site after SCP-2000 introduced Scranton Realty Anchors, which prevent manipulation of reality. Someone then expanded that to the idea to create Humes as a measure of the stability of reality, and asserted that some SCPs mess with reality in a way measurable by Humes. People objected to this on two fronts: 1) having fictional anti-anomaly supertech gave the Foundation a trump card for every anomaly, which is boring, and 2) a lot of people don't canon a single origin or mechanism for all SCPs, which having a measure like Humes implies. In the end, people just kind of headcanoned the devices or not, but people will vote based on that preference.</div>
</div>

							</div>

								
