			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2618112">
									

<p>I look at it like this:</p>
<p>Just because you know <em>that</em> something works, and that it can be used to observe something, doesn't mean you know <em>why</em> it works. And just because things are radically different, doesn't mean they can't be deeply connected.</p>
<p>One of the greatest breakthroughs of the early 1800s? The <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Galvanometer">galvanometer</a>, which allowed the precise measurement of these newly discovered &quot;electrical&quot; phenomena.</p>
<p>One of the greatest breakthroughs of the late 1800's? Figuring out why the hell galvanometers worked, why magnets screwed with them, and why they would sometimes measure currents when they weren't connected to anything: Magnetism and electricity were somehow related as a single &quot;electromagnetism&quot; force.</p>
<p>Breakthrough of the early 1900's? Why they did weird things around those &quot;radio-active&quot; minerals and those weird &quot;cathode tube&quot; things. Electromagnetism became electromagnetic radiation.</p>
<p>Breakthrough of the late 1900's? Finally figuring out that electromagnetic radiation is actually just one part of the electroweak force- which is also responsible for things like friction and the fact that your hand doesn't pass through your keyboard while you're typing. One fundamental force, many manifestations.</p>

							</div>

								
