<p>Scorpion451's analysis is not exactly right, and I suppose that, given how little attention the mechanism of their sensitivity is given in article some clarification is in order, and perhaps an edit. (If/when I do edit this, it will be when I fix the colors and the tone blips, as well)</p>
<p>The intention is not that they are sensitive to some underlying source of anomalies.</p>
<p>Rather, they are sensitive to <em>any change in their environment,</em> just as actual mayflies are sensitive to almost any tampering with their environment, regardless of origin. Along these lines, the article mentions several times that industrial chemicals and natural radiation and temperature changes will effect them just the same as foreign thoughts and warped spacetime</p>
<p>There is not just one thing they are sensitive to, that was never the concept; rather, as an indicator species, they are tied to the health of their environment, and sensitive to anything that is not part of it.</p>
<p>Ergo, they are not sensitive to something because it is anomalous.</p>
<p>They are sensitive to it because it affects them and the space they inhabit in a way that they are not prepared to compensate for, regardless of what it is.</p>
<p>On another note, your engineered concept is an interesting take on it and I may consider a rewrite along those lines some time in the future</p>

